ClassicalMechanics/en/117.response:
\[
r\;\equiv\;\frac{A_{\rm reflected}}{A_{\rm transmitted}}
     \;=\;\frac{k_{1}-k_{2}}{2k_{1}}
     \;=\;\frac{\sqrt{\eta_{1}}-\sqrt{\eta_{2}}}{2\sqrt{\eta_{1}}}
     \;=\;\frac12\!\Bigl(1-\sqrt{\tfrac{\eta_{2}}{\eta_{1}}}\Bigr)\!,
\]

where \(k_i=\omega\sqrt{\eta_i/T}\) is the wave‑number in string \(i\) (\(T\) is the common tension).  

The sign of \(r\) fixes the relative phase: a positive \(r\) means the reflected wave is in phase with the transmitted wave, whereas a negative \(r\) corresponds to a \(\pi\) phase reversal.  

For \(\eta_{2}<\eta_{1}\) we have \(\sqrt{\eta_{2}/\eta_{1}}<1\), so \(r>0\); thus the reflected and transmitted waves are **in phase** (no phase inversion).

